  "version": 354,
  "released": "2026-09-25",
  "changelog": "v354: a reading is only superseded by the same reader — an answer never retires the listener's reading of the same message. WHERE IT WAS SEEN: the owner's own vault, on a fresh clone, while v353 was being verified; v352 SHIPPED IT and v353 filed it rather than swallowing it (lifehug#409), because `system/temporal_store.py` was untouched by that branch. The platform pin is going to v353 and staging's daily maintenance scheduler stays PAUSED until the placement path is trustworthy end to end — this is the last thing gating it, and the audit is now EMPTY. THE DEFECT: `temporal_store._fold` step 1 grouped receipts by `(source_id, revision)`, elected the latest by `receipt_sort_key` as the group's WINNER, and marked every other receipt's claims `superseded / reextracted` — \"a previous interpretation of the same words\". That rule was written for one extractor re-running over one document and it is right for that. But v352's `answer_placement.file_answer` writes its receipt on the promoted reply ITSELF, at that reply's own revision, so on a message the `general_listener` had already read the answer's receipt was simply newer and won the group — and the listener's WHOLE reading of the same words was retired. The loss is one-sided in the worst way: `place-answers` files at most ONE claim per message, so a listener reading that found a date was replaced by one `occurrence`. Two of his answered messages were in that state. `conversation:msg-99efe6688bbd9e4f752438e0` — \"She graduated in 2006\" — had `receipt:537be58a3848123630f24b32` (`general_listener`) retired by `receipt:d9a21bab13bbd169561adc7e` (`answer-placement`), taking `claim:36bae1eb0bbfd2120274471d`, a `date`/`graduation` dated 2006 with subject mention `Katie Ann Merrill`, and with it `node:091ec8a898daa8548afaf104` PLACED AT 2006 and the card that named it; `conversation:msg-c9d84f13e02d1c9547bafaa6` lost `claim:257f9e5681424bdd8539c76a`, a `relative_order` after \"left Kristen\", and the undated `node:3d1065f366b17440103c9a25`. So the v340/v342 placement audit over the whole act read `{\"lost\": [\"node:091ec8a898daa8548afaf104\"], \"moved\": [], \"drawn_at_an_alias\": []}` — the one thing v353 could not get to zero. THE RULE (`temporal_store.A_READING_IS_ONLY_SUPERSEDED_BY_THE_SAME_READER`): the election unit is `(source_id, revision, extractor identity)`. A receipt supersedes another only when the SAME READER re-read the same revision of the same document; two extractors reading one message are two readings of it, both legitimate, and neither retires the other — which is what `answer_placement`'s own receipt has said in prose since v352 and what `temporal_claims.CLAIM_IDENTITY_KEYS` already made true of their CLAIMS. Only the group key was missing it. WHO a reader is, is the NAME at the head of its extractor version (`temporal_claims.extractor_identity` / `AN_EXTRACTORS_NAME_IS_WHO_READ_IT`, one derivation); everything after the name says which VERSION of that reader did the reading. Both spellings this vault files are handled — `name/schema:1/prompt:…/model:…` and the older `name:N` (`classifier_context.EXTRACTOR_VERSION`). AND NOT THE WHOLE `extractor_version`, which the issue suggested first and the code refuses: `temporal_claims.receipt_relative_path` already writes one receipt file per `(source, revision, extractor_version)`, so two receipts in one group ALWAYS differ in that string and keying on it would make every group a singleton and supersession-by-re-extraction dead code. Re-extraction in this vault IS a version bump — a prompt edit mints a new `prompt_version` (`general_listener.PROMPT_VERSION_LENGTH`: \"a prompt edit is a NEW extractor\"), a better reading of the same bytes mints a new rule version (`timeline_evidence.CLASSIFIER_CLAIMS_RULE_VERSION`'s own \"4\" -> \"5\") — so the version stays out of the key and the name goes in, and both of those still supersede exactly as before. IT IS GENERAL AND NOT A SPECIAL CASE FOR `answer_placement`: every pair of readers that can meet on one source revision now coexists — `general_listener` and `landmark_recorder` on one message (`landmark_recorder.file_claims` files both roads in one call and its own docstring already called that corroboration and not duplication), `classifier-claims` and `resolver`, and `landmark-record` and `legacy-entry-import` on one landmark entry, that pair being the SAME deterministic rule under two names whose flip is gated on `landmark_projection.already_substrate_backed` rather than on one retiring the other. The resolver never depended on the election at all: it retires an earlier reading with an explicit `supersede` correction, \"the new receipt stands beside it, never over it\" — which is the behaviour the whole fold now has. `CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION` DOES NOT MOVE, and it is PROVED rather than argued: two fresh scratch clones of the owner's vault at the same head, one with v353's bytes and one with v354's, publish BYTE-IDENTICAL `calculated-timeline.json` and `work-items.json` (generation 188, 1265 nodes, 69 work items, sha256 `661124fc…` and `bc4d8ab6…`). The number describes what the same claims CALCULATE to; this release changes which claims are ACTIVE, and only on a source revision two readers have both read — measured, not one of the owner's 8525 readings at that head. The arithmetic over an active set is untouched and the fold is re-run in full on every publish, so no vault carries a stale index across this change. The index's own shape gains two FIELDS — `extractor_identity` on a source row and `readings` in the counts, beside `sources`, which still counts source revisions — and moves nothing an older reader looked at, so `INDEX_VERSION` stays 1. MEASURED on two fresh scratch clones of the owner's vault at one head (`place-answers` + `publish`, a full publish before and after so both readings are the same kind; the original was never written to). Both read the same 28 answers carrying a card and file the same 8 receipts — 1 placed from the `recency_and_capture_date` reading, 7 `telling_only`, 20 refused `card_not_published`, no errors — and then they diverge, and only here: the placement audit is `{\"lost\": [\"node:091ec8a898daa8548afaf104\"], \"moved\": [], \"drawn_at_an_alias\": []}` under v353 and EMPTY under v354; claims newly retired as `reextracted` go 2 -> 0; nodes go 1265 -> 1263 under v353 and 1265 -> 1265 under v354; placed nodes 1215 -> 1215 against 1215 -> 1216; open cards 69 -> 66 against 69 -> 68. The two nodes v353 loses and v354 keeps are `node:091ec8a898daa8548afaf104` \"Katie Ann Merrill\" at 2006 and the episode `node:3d1065f366b17440103c9a25` \"moving in with dad\", and the two cards that come back with them are `work:89892041c9cdd3d80b0675ef` and `work:ba396d81db81f8c59a630129`. RE-MEASURED at a LATER head of his vault (generation 190 -> 191, a clone taken after the first pair) to prove the fix is not head-specific: the audit is EMPTY again, no claim's status changes over the act (superseded stays 8414), nodes stay 1265, placed nodes go 1218 -> 1219, and the same 2 source revisions are read by 2 readers each — `conversation:msg-99efe6688bbd9e4f752438e0` and `conversation:msg-c9d84f13e02d1c9547bafaa6`, `answer-placement` beside `general_listener` on both. GUARDS: `tests/test_v354_a_reading_is_only_superseded_by_the_same_reader.py` — 32 tests whose part-1 ids are the OWNER'S OWN, minted rather than copied, because his reply's bytes promote to `sources/conversations/msg-99efe6688bbd9e4f752438e0.md` with the same `content_sha256`, so the same listener claim (`claim:36bae1eb0bbfd2120274471d`), the same two receipt ids and the same 2006 node (`node:091ec8a898daa8548afaf104`) fall out of content addressing; nothing reads his vault. The defect REPRODUCED first — the whole file run against v353's bytes fails 21 tests, and the synthetic whole-act audit fails with HIS node id in `lost`. The file also asserts the conditions it needs rather than assuming them (the two receipts share one source revision and the answer's is the newer one, so a future change to receipt ordering fails this file loudly instead of quietly folding the defect away), pins `extractor_identity` over every extractor version the vault files — read from the modules rather than typed out — proves no two filers share an identity, proves a version bump in either spelling still elects a winner, proves three readers of one revision all keep their reading, proves the landmark rule and the legacy import are two readers, proves the fold is still a function of the receipts on disk with two readers in the vault, and runs the whole act through the real sweep and the real publisher with the card minted by `temporal_publication.publish`. THREE COMMITTED FIXTURES CHANGED, because they expressed \"a second interpretation\" as a second EXTRACTOR NAME and the old fold let them: `tests/test_temporal_store.py`'s re-extraction test and randomized-invariant seed now re-read with the same reader at a new prompt version (which is what re-extraction is on this vault) and gain a test that two readers of one message both keep their reading; `tests/test_receipt_directory_inventory.py` re-extracts as `classifier:2` rather than as a differently-named reader; and `tests/test_eras_e2.py`'s Charlee-letter fixture now has the ENTRY state the date it is about — it read 2022 only because the imported reading retired the recorder's reading of the entry it cites, and the two now agree and strengthen ONE placement, which is the shape the fold was built for. Full local suite: 6818 tests, 6817 passed, 1 failed (the known macOS-only `test_file_answer_outer_queue_leaves_no_plaintext_temp`, which fails identically on a pristine tree because `python3` is a broken Xcode shim here), 4 skipped. Ruff parity held exactly: `temporal_store.py`, `temporal_claims.py` and the three edited test files each match their v353 finding CLASSES with nothing new on either side, and the new test file carries only classes its v353 sibling already carries.",
